  "slides": [
    {
      "page_title": "SIGTERM vs SIGKILL: Shutdown Done Right",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "systemd: KillSignal=SIGTERM then SIGKILL",
            "k8s order: preStop hook, TERM, wait, KILL",
            "The OOM killer sends SIGKILL, so no app logs",
            "That is why OOM deaths show exit code 137"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "In Go: signal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGTERM)",
            "Then srv.Shutdown(ctx) to drain connections",
            "Those Sig* hex masks show block/ignore/catch"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "One Syscall, Many Faces",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "kill 1234          # SIGTERM by default",
            "kill -15 1234      # same thing",
            "kill -TERM 1234    # same, and readable"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "kill(pid, sig) is the syscall; the shell's kill is normally a builtin wrapping it."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The pid Argument",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "pid",
            "Targets"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "> 0",
              "that one process"
            ],
            [
              "0",
              "caller's process group"
            ],
            [
              "-1",
              "everything you may signal"
            ],
            [
              "< -1",
              "process group -pid"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Probing And Groups"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "kill -0 1234        # exists + permitted?",
            "kill -- -4711       # whole process group",
            "kill -TERM -$(ps -o pgid= -p 1234)"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "kill -0 sends nothing, only runs the checks",
            "Signalling the group takes down all children"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Permissions"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Your real or effective UID must match theirs",
            "Match is against their real or saved UID",
            "Root may signal anything",
            "Otherwise the call fails with EPERM"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Matching By Name",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "pgrep -a -f 'worker'   # preview matches",
            "pkill -f 'worker'      # then send",
            "killall nginx          # exact name only"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "pkill -f matches the whole command line",
            "Always pgrep before you pkill",
            "killall means something else on some Unixes",
            "So prefer pkill in portable scripts"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Blast Radius",
          "text": "pkill -f java on a shared box kills every JVM there. Scope the match with -u <user>, --ns <pid>, or target an exact cgroup."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Grace For Free"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "timeout -s TERM -k 30 300 ./job.sh",
            "# TERM at 300s, KILL 30s later"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Sending Signals: kill, pkill and Friends",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Self And Modern"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "raise(sig) signals the calling process",
            "abort() raises SIGABRT to dump core on purpose"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "c",
          "lines": [
            "int fd = pidfd_open(pid, 0);",
            "pidfd_send_signal(fd, SIGTERM, NULL, 0);",
            "// no PID-reuse race window at all"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Hierarchy",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "session",
              "process group",
              "process"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "A session holds one or more process groups; each group holds one or more processes. This is the plumbing job control runs on."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Groups Receive Signals"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "The group is the terminal's unit of delivery",
            "Ctrl-C sends SIGINT to the whole foreground group",
            "Not to one process — to every member",
            "The shell puts each pipeline in its own new group",
            "So a | b | c is three processes, one group"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "setsid() Cuts The Cord"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Creates a new session, caller becomes leader",
            "Caller also becomes process group leader",
            "Detaches from any controlling terminal",
            "This is the core move in daemonising"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Foreground vs Background",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Group",
            "Terminal access"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "foreground",
              "reads and writes freely"
            ],
            [
              "background read",
              "gets SIGTTIN, stops"
            ],
            [
              "background write",
              "SIGTTOU if stty tostop"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "One foreground group per controlling terminal",
            "tcgetpgrp / tcsetpgrp read and set it",
            "A 'frozen' background job is usually stopped by TTIN"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Hang-Up Propagation"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Closing the terminal makes the kernel send SIGHUP",
            "It goes to the session leader first",
            "The leader typically propagates it to all jobs",
            "That is how an SSH drop kills long-running work"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Surviving The Hang-Up",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "nohup ./job.sh &      # ignore SIGHUP",
            "setsid ./job.sh       # leave the session",
            "disown -h %1          # mark hangup-immune",
            "tmux new -d './job.sh'  # best option"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A systemd unit is the durable version of this",
            "It survives reboots, not just logouts"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Sending Signals: kill, pkill and Friends (cont.)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Why Daemons Double-Fork"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "fork, parent exits, child calls setsid()",
            "The child forks again and the middle one exits",
            "The grandchild can never acquire a terminal",
            "It is orphaned on purpose, so init reaps it"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Reading The Relations"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "ps -eo pid,pgid,sid,tty,stat,comm",
            "# STAT '+' = foreground group member",
            "# STAT 's' = session leader"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "The ? Column",
          "text": "A TTY column showing ? means no controlling terminal — the signature of a properly daemonised process."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "A Job Is A Pipeline",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A job is the shell's handle on one pipeline",
            "Numbered %1, %2, %3 as you create them",
            "%% and %+ are current, %- is previous",
            "jobs -l lists them with PIDs and states"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Moving Jobs Around"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Action",
            "Signal sent"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Ctrl-Z",
              "SIGTSTP, state T"
            ],
            [
              "bg %1",
              "SIGCONT, stays背 background"
            ],
            [
              "fg %1",
              "SIGCONT + terminal handoff"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "./job.sh &      # start in background",
            "jobs -l         # [1]+ 4711 Running",
            "fg %1           # bring it forward"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Waiting On Children"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "$! holds the PID of the last background job",
            "wait blocks for every background job",
            "wait $! blocks for that single one",
            "wait -n returns as soon as any job finishes"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Detaching",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "disown %1 drops the job from the shell's table",
            "It then never receives the shell's SIGHUP",
            "disown -h keeps it listed but hangup-immune"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "long_task > out.log 2>&1 &",
            "disown            # now safe to log out",
            "# without it, SIGHUP ends the work"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Keys Are Just Signals"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Key",
            "Signal"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Ctrl-C",
              "SIGINT"
            ],
            [
              "Ctrl-\\",
              "SIGQUIT"
            ],
            [
              "Ctrl-Z",
              "SIGTSTP"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "All three are configured per terminal",
            "stty -a prints the current key bindings"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "trap In Scripts"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "trap 'cleanup' EXIT INT TERM",
            "trap -p            # list installed traps",
            "trap '' TERM       # ignore it outright"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        }
      ],
      "cont": true
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Process Groups, Sessions and Terminals",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Forwarding, Properly",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "./child.sh & child=$!",
            "trap 'kill -TERM $child; wait $child' TERM",
            "wait $child        # first wait returns early",
            "wait $child        # second one really waits"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A shell does not forward signals automatically",
            "The wrapper dies, the real work keeps running",
            "exec cmd replaces the shell and fixes it outright",
            "wait returns immediately when a trap fires"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "No Job Control Here",
          "text": "Non-interactive shells have job control off by default — that is why fg inside a script fails with 'no job control in this shell'."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Reaching For -9 First",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Skips cleanup and corrupts on-disk state",
            "Hides the real reason the process hung",
            "Always TERM, wait, then escalate"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Doing Work In A Handler"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "c",
          "lines": [
            "void bad(int s) { printf(\"bye\\n\"); }  // no",
            "void ok(int s)  { got_sig = 1; }      // yes",
            "// printf/malloc can deadlock on locks"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "A SIGCHLD That Runs Once"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Standard signals coalesce on delivery",
            "One SIGCHLD may cover several dead children",
            "Loop with WNOHANG until it returns 0 or -1",
            "Otherwise zombies accumulate silently"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Forking A Dirty Buffer"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "c",
          "lines": [
            "fflush(NULL);   // do this first",
            "fork();         // else output prints twice"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Shell Form In Docker"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "CMD myapp makes /bin/sh the PID 1",
            "The shell swallows SIGTERM, app never sees it",
            "Every stop then burns the full 10s timeout",
            "Use exec form CMD [\"myapp\"] instead"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Treating EINTR As Fatal"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Without SA_RESTART, any signal interrupts I/O",
            "read/accept/select return -1 with EINTR",
            "Retry the call, do not abort the connection"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Killing A Stale PID"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "ps -p 4711 -o lstart=   # verify start time",
            "# or hold a pidfd instead of an integer"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Job Control in the Shell",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Leaking fds Across exec"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Children inherit your sockets and log files",
            "The port stays bound after the parent restarts",
            "Set O_CLOEXEC on everything you don't pass on"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Ignoring A Signal Into A Child",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "c",
          "lines": [
            "signal(SIGINT, SIG_IGN);  // sticks",
            "execvp(argv[0], argv);    // still ignored"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Handlers reset to default across exec",
            "But an ignored disposition survives it",
            "So the child cannot be Ctrl-C'd at all"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Finding The Killer",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "echo $?              # 128+N names the signal",
            "dmesg | grep -i oom  # OOM kills land here",
            "journalctl -u myapp  # unit-level history",
            "coredumpctl info     # the core itself"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Watching It Live"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "strace -f -e trace=%process,%signal -p 4711",
            "watch -n1 'ps -o pid,stat,wchan= -p 4711'",
            "cat /proc/4711/wchan; cat /proc/4711/stack"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "wchan names the blocking kernel function",
            "For D state the fix is on the I/O path",
            "Dead disk or hung NFS, never the signal itself"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Safe Lab",
          "text": "sleep 300 & then try kill -STOP, -CONT, -TERM, -9 in turn and watch STAT walk S, T, S, then gone."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Only Two Verbs",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Creation is always fork, clone or posix_spawn",
            "Running a new program is always exec",
            "Nothing else makes a process on Linux"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Call",
            "Returns",
            "Keeps"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "fork",
              "twice",
              "everything, lazily (COW)"
            ],
            [
              "exec",
              "never, on success",
              "the PID only"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Lifecycle"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "fork",
              "exec",
              "run",
              "exit",
              "zombie",
              "parent waits",
              "gone"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Skip the wait and the zombie stays. That is the whole bug, every time."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Common Mistakes & Debugging Playbook",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Exit Codes Decoded",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Code",
            "Means"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "0",
              "success"
            ],
            [
              "1-125",
              "app defined"
            ],
            [
              "126",
              "not executable"
            ],
            [
              "127",
              "not found"
            ],
            [
              "128+N",
              "killed by N"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "sh -c 'exit 42'; echo $?     # 42",
            "sh -c 'kill -9 $$'; echo $?  # 137",
            "# 143 = 128+15 = SIGTERM"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "$? holds the last command's exit status",
            "${PIPESTATUS[@]} holds every stage of a pipe",
            "An exit code is only 8 bits wide"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Read 137 Right",
          "text": "137 is 128+9, so SIGKILL — usually the OOM killer. 143 is 128+15, a SIGTERM somebody sent on purpose."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Four Identity Numbers"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "PID identifies you inside a PID namespace",
            "PPID names your parent",
            "PGID groups a pipeline, SID groups the session",
            "Signals travel along exactly those groupings"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Signals In One Breath",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Async notifications with three dispositions",
            "Default, ignore, or your own handler",
            "Standard signals coalesce when blocked",
            "Real-time signals queue and keep their order"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Signal",
            "Nature"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "SIGTERM",
              "asks politely"
            ],
            [
              "SIGKILL",
              "just takes"
            ],
            [
              "SIGSTOP",
              "just takes"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "The Uncatchable Pair",
          "text": "SIGKILL and SIGSTOP are the only two signals that cannot be caught, blocked or ignored. Ask first, wait a grace period, then take."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Summary: The Whole Model",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Dead vs Orphaned"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Zombies waste PIDs, never memory",
            "Fix the parent's reaping, not the symptom",
            "Orphans keep running and are adopted",
            "Adopted by PID 1 or the nearest subreaper"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Kind",
            "Costs",
            "Fix"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "zombie",
              "one PID slot",
              "parent reaps"
            ],
            [
              "orphan",
              "nothing",
              "init adopts"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Job Control Recap",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "It is signals + process groups + a terminal",
            "Ctrl-C = SIGINT to the foreground group",
            "Ctrl-Z = SIGTSTP to the same group",
            "bg/fg = SIGCONT plus a terminal handoff"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Handler Discipline"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Set a flag, write a byte, return",
            "Do the real work back in the main loop",
            "Or take delivery synchronously with signalfd"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Reaper Loop",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "c",
          "lines": [
            "while (waitpid(-1, &st, WNOHANG) > 0)",
            "    ;   // until it returns 0 or -1"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "One SIGCHLD can cover several dead children",
            "SIG_IGN auto-reaps but discards every status"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Toolkit To Keep"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "ps -eo pid,ppid,pgid,sid,stat,comm",
            "pstree -p ; kill -l ; kill -0 <pid>",
            "pgrep -a ; strace -f -e %signal",
            "cat /proc/<pid>/status ; timeout -k"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Ship Rule",
          "text": "For anything you deploy: handle SIGTERM, reap your children, set CLOEXEC, and never assume you are not PID 1."
        }
      ]
    }
